# Platform staging example environment template. Copy it to a private path OUTSIDE the checkout,
# fill every empty value, keep it mode 0600, and pass it with `docker compose --env-file`.
# Empty is a deliberate value here: every required variable below is a `${VAR:?}` in the three
# manifests this composition pulls in (the platform component, the stage fragment and
# components/control/synthetics/compose.yaml, which synthetics component added), so an unfilled install fails at
# `config` time rather than starting something unspecified. A line that carries a NON-empty value is
# the `${VAR:-default}` shape: the manifests boot without it, and the value shown is what they boot
# as (LO_GATUS_URL, LO_DETECTION_WINDOW_SECONDS and everything under "Optional"). Two further defaulted
# variables carry no line here because the stage wants the default: LO_PLATFORM_URL
# (http://platform:8002) and LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP (1, the plaintext project network the detector and
# the engine talk over — the fragment hardcoded that value before synthetics component, the manifest defaults it).
#
# This stage records and never sends. `LO_NOTIFICATION_MODE` below is the mode the platform boots
# with, and the manifest default is `recording`; the gate refuses a default of `live`, so a stage
# that means to reach a human channel has to say so here and mean it.

# --- Images ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both manifests set `pull_policy: never`, so each value must already exist in the local daemon.
# Third-party image: repository@sha256:<64 lowercase hex>, resolved for linux/amd64 by you.
# Candidate version: components/control/synthetics/versions.json records the release tag the pin was
# read against (v5.36.0), the registry URL it was read from and the date, plus the moving-channel
# digest this component supersedes. Pin the value it names as `image`, not a tag.
LO_GATUS_IMAGE=
# First-party image, built from this checkout (docs/BUILD.md): the image config ID printed by
# `docker build -q` / `docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' <name>`, not a registry digest.
# Three services run it here -- the platform API, the notification sink and the detector.
LO_PLATFORM_IMAGE=

# --- Read-only binds the stage fragment adds ----------------------------------------------------
# Absolute directory holding the reviewed conformance scripts the notification sink and the stage
# probe execute (`scripts/notification_sink.py`, `scripts/platform_probe.py`). Mounted read-only at
# /checks in both, so the stage never runs code it did not put there.
LO_PLATFORM_CHECKS_DIR=
# Absolute directory containing the compiled rule and job files the detector posts about; the stage
# driver builds it beside the index. Read-only at /rules.
LO_DETECTION_RULE_DIR=
# Absolute path of the Gatus config this stage watches (its own synthetic endpoint only, never a
# production target). examples/platform/gatus.yaml is the starting point; copy it out first, because
# a stage that edits the checkout edits its own evidence. Leave this line empty for the driven stage:
# scripts/conformance_platform_stage.py writes its own rendered copy (that document plus a
# `security.basic` block holding the bcrypt hash of the run's synthetic password) inside the work tree
# and names that file instead — this template's value is for a manual bring-up, which is
# operator-configured. The component's own shape — with the `security:` block the committed example
# deliberately does NOT carry — is components/control/synthetics/config.yaml; see the note on
# LO_GATUS_TOKEN_FILE below.
LO_GATUS_CONFIG=
# Which Gatus endpoint this stage's detector reads, as its DERIVED key: group `staging` + name
# `synthetic-http` -> `staging_synthetic-http` (upstream's config/key/key.go sanitises each half and
# joins them with `_`). The manifest defaults this to the product config's own endpoint, so a stage
# with its own Gatus document has to name its own key — a key that does not exist answers 404 and the
# detector files coverage, which is the loud failure rather than a quiet one.
LO_GATUS_URL=http://gatus:8080/api/v1/endpoints/staging_synthetic-http/statuses
# Evaluation cadence in seconds, 5..3600 (the manifest's default, and what the stage has always run
# at, is 5). Out of range or non-integer and the detector refuses to start.
LO_DETECTION_WINDOW_SECONDS=5

# --- Built artefacts the platform reads ---------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute directory holding the inventory snapshot (inventory.db) the platform joins events
# against; mounted read-only at /inventory by both the component and the detector.
LO_PLATFORM_INDEX_DIR=
# Absolute directory containing the action policy file actions.json (the platform reads
# /config/actions.json from it) and, when you turn on live delivery, the notify-token file the
# component mounts at /config/notify-token. examples/platform/actions.json is the starting point.
LO_PLATFORM_POLICY_DIR=

# --- Credentials, as paths only ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every line here names a FILE holding one credential, never the credential itself. An environment
# value is readable by anyone who can run `docker inspect` or read /proc/<pid>/environ of the
# container, so each manifest mounts the file as a Compose secret and the container sees only the
# path inside it. The three one-value files below are declared by two manifests now: `notify-token` at
# the foot of examples/platform/staging.compose.yaml, `gatus-token` and `producer-token` at the foot of
# components/control/synthetics/compose.yaml. The stage driver creates them with `printf '%s'`, no
# trailing newline, mode 0600 (scripts/conformance_platform_stage.py). Generate each value
# separately -- one value shared across roles defeats the role model.
# Absolute path to the platform role credentials file: a JSON list of {identity, role, token}
# objects, mounted at /run/secrets/platform-credentials. Roles are reader, producer, proposer,
# human, executor, summary; stage rows identify `stage-*` actors, never the owner.
LO_PLATFORM_CREDENTIALS_FILE=
# Bearer token the notification sink accepts, and the one the stage probe presents to the platform.
# Same value on both sides of that pair: the sink is the channel, the probe is the sender.
LO_NOTIFY_TOKEN_FILE=
# Bearer token the detector presents to the platform intake endpoint: the detector's OWN producer
# credential . It must be the `token` of a producer-role row in LO_PLATFORM_CREDENTIALS_FILE whose `identity`
# equals the `source:` line of the rule document in LO_DETECTION_RULE_DIR (the shipped stage rule says
# `stage-detector`, and the stage driver mints that row), since the platform refuses an event whose
# source differs from the identity its token authenticated as.
LO_DETECTOR_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE=
# The credential the detector presents to Gatus. Separate from the two above: a probe credential that
# also opens the platform API is one leaked value instead of one. Its CONTENT RULE changed under
# synthetics component: the adapter now sends HTTP Basic, because that is the only authentication the pinned Gatus
# release offers on the status route (components/control/synthetics/CONTRACT.md cites the files), and
# Basic wants base64("<user>:<password>"), not a bearer token. That is a content rule and not a
# suggestion: a bearer-shaped file is refused at the read, before the first request.
#
# WHO ENFORCES IT depends on which stage this is. A fresh stage run by
# scripts/conformance_platform_stage.py mints one synthetic principal, hashes its password with the
# documented `htpasswd -niBC 9 <user>` stdin form (a prerequisite of that run, never installed by it),
# mounts the rendered authenticated copy of the Gatus document, and refuses before any service starts
# if the tool is missing or answers unrecognisably — so on that stage the engine really does check this
# file's contents. The manual example this template describes is the opposite case: an engine started
# from examples/platform/gatus.yaml carries no `security:` block, which at the pinned release means
# Gatus registers no auth middleware and answers anyone on the project network, so on THAT stage these
# bytes are sent and ignored, exactly as a bearer token used to be. Closing it is the operator's own
# edit — render the component's config.yaml with both marked lines — and it is deliberately not
# quietly switched on here, because the live host runs this artefact under someone else's feet.
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$(printf '%s:%s' 'lo-detector' "$password" | base64 -w0)" > gatus-token
LO_GATUS_TOKEN_FILE=

# --- Optional settings ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Delivery posture: off | recording | live. Recording stores attempts and sends nothing; live
# reaches the human channel and is never the default here.
LO_NOTIFICATION_MODE=recording
# Optional JSON notification-safety policy (budgets, synthetic sources, test window). If it declares
# delivery_mode, that value must equal LO_NOTIFICATION_MODE or the platform refuses to boot.
LO_NOTIFICATION_POLICY=
# Optional JSON label/display mapping applied when formatting human-facing messages.
LO_DISPLAY_CONFIG=
# Optional observation document summarised by GET /v1/overview.
LO_OVERVIEW_PATH=
# Webhook channel, honoured in live mode only. Leave empty to keep delivery off.
LO_NOTIFY_URL=
# Set to 1 only while the channel is plaintext HTTP on an isolated project network.
LO_NOTIFY_ALLOW_HTTP=0
# Telegram channel config JSON path (token, chat_id); honoured in live mode only, and it competes
# with LO_NOTIFY_URL -- configure exactly one channel.
LO_TELEGRAM_CONFIG=
# The platform API, published on loopback only. 18083 is the address the live stage has used from
# the start; change it if another stack on this host already holds it.
LO_PLATFORM_PORT=18083
